#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125365} :activity/id #uuid "391e2b89-8cde-5a13-8210-4ec865147d71" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049923 :topic/id #uuid "1ed8dd8d-e728-5e57-81fe-a7c1a6a08469" :topic/math-academy-id 1927} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125338 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124481 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124294 :question/id #uuid "35290ca2-1488-52ba-9d2e-b8c3e04c45e4" :question/math-academy-id "e-4743"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "202575c6-7672-5387-a5ae-762bef520add" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050358} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050404}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125205} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125210}] :knowledge-point/title "Separating a Square Root Into a Product of Two Square Roots"} :step/id #uuid "8ba8ca19-5df4-53c5-ab7a-0a7ba56e0bc7" :step/math-academy-id 17274 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125339}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125339 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124482 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124295 :question/id #uuid "204328e4-810f-591e-a085-bb9cdadca875" :question/math-academy-id "e-4744"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d6f8c2c9-e82f-5d37-9f34-4811dd88c78d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125206} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125207}] :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Product of Two Square Roots as a Single Square Root"} :step/id #uuid "ec31ad60-33b1-5f33-bc69-7dda09da6514" :step/math-academy-id 17275 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125367}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125340 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124483 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124296 :question/id #uuid "0c797a57-00a8-57b9-af30-655e8952c4c3" :question/math-academy-id "e-4756"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e97098b0-a7f9-564a-899e-60fb88682c80" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048770}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125209}] :knowledge-point/title "The Product Rule With Cube Roots"} :step/id #uuid "48f71bf2-cfc9-583f-81af-bf3c85f247e7" :step/math-academy-id 17346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125365 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125769 :tutorial/content "To multiply square roots, we simply multiply the numbers under the root. This is called the **product rule for radicals**.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's simplify the expression $\\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{5}$ by applying the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{5} = \\sqrt{2 \\cdot 5} = \\sqrt{10}\n$$\n\nThe product rule also works for cube roots. For example,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{2} \\cdot \\sqrt[3]{5} = \\sqrt[3]{2 \\cdot 5} = \\sqrt[3]{10}\n$$\n\nIn general, the product rule works for any product of $n$th roots. So for any $n$, we have\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[n]{2} \\cdot \\sqrt[n]{5} = \\sqrt[n]{2 \\cdot 5} = \\sqrt[n]{10}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** We cannot use the product rule when we have the product of different radicals, such as square roots and cube roots. For example, the expression $\\sqrt{2} \\times \\sqrt[3]{5}$ cannot be simplified because the radicals $\\sqrt{\\phantom{x}}$ and $\\sqrt[3]{\\phantom{x}}$ are different." :tutorial/id #uuid "f7f60ab3-2c97-5570-bacc-49ef3d371c52" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3296 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "dcc53fd7-b344-545d-8cd9-408d76d4ce85" :step/math-academy-id 18176 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125366}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125366 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125770 :tutorial/content "The product rule can often be used to simplify square roots.\n\nFor example, consider $\\sqrt{98}$. Note that\n\n$$\n98=49 \\cdot 2\n$$\n\nwhere $49$ is a perfect square. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{98} = \\sqrt{49 \\cdot 2} = \\sqrt{49} \\cdot \\sqrt{2} = 7 \\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nAlternatively, if we're given a simplified square root and we want to convert it to the square root of a single number, then we can work backwards using the product rule:\n\n$$\n7 \\sqrt{2} = \\sqrt{49} \\cdot \\sqrt{2} = \\sqrt{49 \\cdot 2} = \\sqrt{98}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6b8006c0-4a18-5c9d-84c6-1224cf85a921" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3298 :tutorial/title "Simplifying Square Roots"} :step/id #uuid "85a7257b-e917-5ef4-854d-85d0f5b7753b" :step/math-academy-id 18178 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125338}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125367 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125771 :tutorial/content "The product rule applies to cube roots as well.\n\nFor example, consider $\\sqrt[3]{24}$. Note that\n\n$$\n24=8 \\cdot 3\n$$\n\nwhere $8$ is a perfect cube. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{24} = \\sqrt[3]{8 \\cdot 3} = \\sqrt[3]{8} \\cdot \\sqrt[3]{3} = 2 \\sqrt[3]{3}\n$$\n\nAgain, if we're given a simplified cube root and we want to convert it to the cube root of a single number then we can work backwards using the product rule:\n\n$$\n2 \\sqrt[3]{3} = \\sqrt[3]{8} \\cdot \\sqrt[3]{3} = \\sqrt[3]{8 \\cdot 3} = \\sqrt[3]{24}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d8fc754e-e350-5273-9188-9203a384b9b6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3299 :tutorial/title "The Product Rule With Cube Roots"} :step/id #uuid "e9b6616d-b6fe-5529-bc7e-89b4bc69c83e" :step/math-academy-id 18179 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125340}}] :activity/title "The Product Rule for Radicals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124720}]}
